import{_ as t,o,c as a,ag as s}from"./chunks/framework.D1z9wg1y.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"Permissions","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"safeguards/permissions.md","filePath":"safeguards/permissions.md"}'),d={name:"safeguards/permissions.md"};function n(r,e,c,i,l,h){return o(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<h1 id="permissions" tabindex="-1">Permissions <a class="header-anchor" href="#permissions" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Permissions&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Before a tool call runs, Claude Code compares it with three lists of rules: <code>allow</code>, <code>ask</code> and <code>deny</code>. A deny refuses the call, an ask shows you a prompt, and an allow lets it run with no prompt. The harness writes the three lists from the settings template <code>settings/settings.proposal.json</code>. This page gives the principle behind each list, the main groups of rules, and how the lists reach the other frontends.</p><h2 id="the-principle-of-the-three-lists" tabindex="-1">The principle of the three lists <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-principle-of-the-three-lists" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The principle of the three lists&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Most rules of the template state no reason of their own. One principle sorts them:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">list</th><th style="text-align:left;">a command goes here when it is</th><th style="text-align:left;">examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>allow</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">local and reversible: a mistake costs a command to undo</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>git status</code>, <code>git commit</code>, <code>git push origin &lt;branch&gt;</code>, read-only tools, <code>julia</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>ask</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">irreversible or outward-facing: it changes what other people see, or cannot be undone</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>gh pr merge</code>, <code>gh release create</code>, <code>git revert</code>, a remote branch deleted</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>deny</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">destructive, a sweep, a way around a gate, or a read of a secret</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>git reset --hard</code>, <code>git add -A</code>, <code>sed -i</code>, <code>curl</code>, <code>gh auth token</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Claude Code resolves the lists by category, not by order: a deny wins over an ask, and an ask wins over an allow. So a broad allow, such as <code>git push origin *</code>, stays safe as long as the forms that must not run, such as a forced push, have their own deny.</p><h3 id="allow" tabindex="-1">Allow <a class="header-anchor" href="#allow" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Allow&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The <code>allow</code> list holds the everyday commands of the work: the <code>git</code> commands of a change (<code>status</code>, <code>log</code>, <code>add</code>, <code>commit</code>, <code>push origin</code>, <code>rebase</code>, <code>merge</code>, <code>branch -D</code>, and <code>worktree add</code> below <code>~/Research/.worktrees/</code>), the reads of <code>gh</code> and <code>glab</code>, the writes of <code>gh</code> in your own organisations, such as <code>gh pr create -R &lt;org&gt;/*</code>, the read-only tools, <code>julia</code>, and the Kaimon and Zotero MCP tools. A rule that names <code>{org}</code> expands to one rule for each organisation of the profile&#39;s <code>org</code> list.</p><p><strong>Under the sandbox, most <code>Bash</code> allows are inert.</strong> The template sets <code>autoAllowBashIfSandboxed</code>, so Claude Code approves every command that runs inside the sandbox without reading <code>allow</code>. An allow matters for the commands that run outside the sandbox, <code>git</code>, <code>gh</code>, <code>glab</code> and <code>ps</code>, and for the tools that are not shell commands.</p><h3 id="ask" tabindex="-1">Ask <a class="header-anchor" href="#ask" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Ask&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The <code>ask</code> list holds the steps that you decide: a merge, a release, the creation, edit or fork of a repository, a deletion on a remote (<code>git push --delete</code>, a push of <code>:&lt;ref&gt;</code>), a local tag deleted or moved (<code>git tag -d</code>, <code>-f</code>), <code>git revert</code>, the <code>git -C</code> forms of these, <code>git archive -o</code>, the <code>gh issue create</code> forms with <code>-R</code> after other arguments and the <code>gh</code> comments that name two repositories or a URL, and an edit of a skill that does not come from the harness. A <code>gh</code> write outside your own organisations matches no <code>allow</code> rule and runs outside the sandbox, so it asks too. A prompt is a real control: you see the exact call, and nothing runs until you answer.</p><h3 id="deny" tabindex="-1">Deny <a class="header-anchor" href="#deny" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Deny&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The <code>deny</code> list holds the forms that are never right in a session:</p><ul><li><strong>In-place editors</strong>: <code>sed -i</code>, <code>perl -pi</code>, <code>ast-grep -U</code>. A shell rewrite reports success whether it matched or not; a session edits a file with its edit tools. <code>fatou format</code> too, because it reformats against the repository&#39;s style.</li><li><strong>Destructive commands</strong>: <code>git reset --hard</code>, <code>git clean -f</code>, a forced or mirror push, a lease push to <code>main</code> or <code>master</code>, <code>gh repo delete</code>, a <code>gh api</code> DELETE, and destructive system commands.</li><li><strong>Sweeps</strong>: <code>git add -A</code>, <code>git commit -a</code>, <code>git commit --amend</code>, and the <code>git stash</code> forms that act on everything that changed (<a href="./git-safety#staging">Git safety</a>).</li><li><strong>Ways around a gate</strong>: <code>git config --global</code>, <code>git -c core.hooksPath=…</code>, the <code>-c</code> forms of <code>credential</code>, <code>http</code>, <code>include</code> and <code>protocol</code>, and <code>git --exec-path</code>.</li><li><strong>The network outside the tools</strong>: <code>curl</code>, <code>ssh</code> and <code>rtk curl</code>. A web read goes through the <code>WebFetch</code> tool, whose hosts <code>harness install</code> adds from <code>settings/network.toml</code> and the profile&#39;s overlay.</li><li><strong>Self-configuration and persistence</strong>: <code>claude mcp</code>, <code>launchctl</code> and <code>crontab</code>, which add a program that runs later, outside every check of the session.</li><li><strong>Secrets</strong>: <code>gh auth token</code>, and <code>Read</code> denies on the files that hold keys and tokens.</li><li><strong>Installed files</strong>: <code>Edit</code> denies on the files that <code>harness install</code> writes into <code>~/.claude/</code>, on the settings, on <code>**/.git/**</code>, <code>.githooks</code>, the shell&#39;s startup files and the tool installs. A session cannot change the rules that bind the next session.</li></ul><p>A <code>Read</code> or <code>Edit</code> deny of a path also goes into the sandbox&#39;s read or write deny list, so it holds for a shell command too (<a href="./sandbox#the-session-sandbox">Sandboxes</a>).</p><h2 id="what-a-rule-cannot-see" tabindex="-1">What a rule cannot see <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-a-rule-cannot-see" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What a rule cannot see&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A rule is a text pattern. It matches the text of the command, not what the command does:</p><ul><li><strong>A command inside a string escapes it.</strong> <code>sh -c &#39;…&#39;</code>, <code>find -exec</code> and <code>julia -e &#39;run(…)&#39;</code> carry a command that no pattern of the outer command sees. The class is open, so a longer deny list does not close it.</li><li><strong>A rule cannot see the state of the repository.</strong> &quot;A forced worktree removal is fine when it loses nothing&quot; depends on the worktree, not on the text. Such a check is a <a href="./guard-hooks">guard hook</a>, which runs code.</li><li><strong>A rule cannot see the directory.</strong> <code>git -C &lt;path&gt; status</code> does not match <code>Bash(git status *)</code>. <code>harness settings twins</code> prints the <code>git -C</code> twin of each plain <code>git</code> rule.</li></ul><p>So the permission lists prevent accidents, and the <a href="./sandbox">sandbox</a> is the boundary (<a href="./security-model">The security model</a>).</p><h2 id="the-permission-modes" tabindex="-1">The permission modes <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-permission-modes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The permission modes&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The template sets <code>defaultMode: default</code>, so an ask shows a prompt, and <code>disableBypassPermissionsMode: disable</code>, so a session cannot turn the lists off. In the <code>auto</code> mode of Claude Code, an ask goes to an auto-mode classifier, which can let the call run with no prompt. A deny still refuses the call in every mode.</p><h2 id="the-other-frontends" tabindex="-1">The other frontends <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-other-frontends" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The other frontends&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The template is written for Claude Code. <code>harness install</code> renders it for each other frontend as far as that frontend&#39;s grammar can say the same:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">frontend</th><th style="text-align:left;">gets</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;">OpenCode</td><td style="text-align:left;">the <code>ask</code> and <code>deny</code> rules, as its permission block; <code>ask</code> first, because OpenCode takes the last match. The <code>allow</code> list is not ported: OpenCode allows by default, and the guard plugin checks the rest</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">oh-my-pi</td><td style="text-align:left;">the <code>Bash</code> rules of all three lists as <code>deny</code>, <code>prompt</code> and <code>allow</code> patterns, and the path list <code>guard-paths.json</code>, with <code>approvalMode: yolo</code>. Its guard extension refuses the paths of the <code>Edit</code> denies and of the <code>Edit</code> asks, which it cannot ask for, and runs the guard checks</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">Antigravity</td><td style="text-align:left;">all three lists, where its grammar can state the rule</td></tr></tbody></table><p><a href="./../harness-command#permissions"><code>harness permissions</code></a> regenerates the OpenCode block and the path list from the template. <a href="./../components/adapters">Adapters</a> describes each rendering.</p><h2 id="change-a-rule" tabindex="-1">Change a rule <a class="header-anchor" href="#change-a-rule" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Change a rule&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Change the template in the harness, never the installed <code>~/.claude/settings.json</code>: the template denies an edit of the installed file. Run <code>harness install</code> and read the diff of the section <code>permissions</code>, then <code>harness install --apply</code>. Test each changed rule in a new session with a call that it must stop and a call that it must let through (<a href="./security-model#check-a-change-of-the-settings">The security model</a>). <code>harness settings surface</code> counts how many recent commands reach a prompt under a settings file, and <code>harness settings compare</code> compares two files.</p>',27)])])}const m=t(d,[["render",n]]);export{p as __pageData,m as default};
